---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Focus from '../components/Focus.astro';
import Demo from '../islands/Demo.tsx';
import Turn from '../components/Turn.astro';
import Numbers from '../components/Numbers.astro';
import Engines from '../components/Engines.astro';
import What from '../components/What.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import Close from '../components/Close.astro';
---
{/* No title and no description here. They are the site's, not this route's:
    ../lib/site owns them because the head, the JSON-LD graph and /llms.txt all
    state them, and a fourth copy typed into the one page that exists is the
    copy that drifts. A second route overrides them; this one is the site. */}
<Base>
  {/* TWO PLANES, AND THE SECOND ONE COVERS THE FIRST.

      The page opens on one claim and then argues it, and until now the two
      shared a scroll: the hero was 434px tall, so at 1440x900 the heading, the
      capsule, the focus band and the next section's heading were all on screen
      at once. Everything was visible and nothing was first. The opening claim
      had no moment of its own and the argument had no beginning.

      So the opening is a plane the page is pinned to, and the argument is a
      plane that slides up over it. The reader scrolls the cover into place
      rather than scrolling the hero away, which is a different gesture and
      makes a different promise: the hero is not gone, it is underneath.

      IT IS `position: sticky` AND NOTHING ELSE. No ScrollTrigger, no
      transform, no listener. The seam is a sticky element and an opaque one
      after it in normal flow, which means it works before any script arrives,
      it works with script disabled, and it works when the reader has asked for
      reduced motion -- because it is not motion. Nothing animates; one box
      stops moving while the box after it keeps going.

      It composes with the rest of the page for the reason `lib/smooth.ts`
      already documents: Lenis animates the real scroll offset, so the reveal
      observer, the header's is-stuck toggle and the anchor jumps are all
      unaware of this. Verified against the built page before it was written:
      a sticky probe in `main` holds at top 0 through a 1500px scroll, so
      `body { overflow-x: hidden }` -- which the ASCII band needs -- does not
      cost sticky here.

      WHAT IS ON WHICH PLANE IS NOT A LAYOUT DETAIL. The hero and the focus
      band are one claim: it lands in the window you already had open, and here
      are the windows. Splitting them would put the evidence on the cover and
      the claim underneath it. The argument starts where the page starts asking
      questions, which is "Where your words go." */}
  <div class="pin">
    <Hero />
    <Focus />
  </div>

  <div class="over">
    <section class="sec" id="how">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sec__head rise">
          <h2>Where your words go.</h2>
          <p>Putting text at the cursor is the easy third. Everyone can do it, and soon
            everyone will do it for free.</p>
        </div>

        <Demo client:visible />
      </div>
    </section>

    <Turn />
    <Numbers />
    <What />
    <Engines />
    <Faq />
    <Close />
  </div>
</Base>
